### Before reporting a bug

- [x] I have searched open and closed [GitHub issues](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere/issues).
- [x] I have read the [ShardingSphere documentation](https://shardingsphere.apache.org/document/current/en/overview).

### ShardingSphere version

master, commit [`01446d4fad4`](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere/commit/01446d4fad48450e7fc3eb3b3631b25d6bf411df)

### ShardingSphere project

ShardingSphere-Proxy

### Expected behavior

The proxy should execute [`SAVEPOINT`](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/firebird/blob/v5.0.4/src/dsql/parse.y#L5310-L5313), [`ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT`](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/firebird/blob/v5.0.4/src/dsql/parse.y#L5324-L5327) and [`RELEASE SAVEPOINT`](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/firebird/blob/v5.0.4/src/dsql/parse.y#L5316-L5321) as the Firebird server does.

### Actual behavior

The proxy rejects each of these statements with an error.

The same statements were executed directly on the Firebird server and through the proxy, one after another in one transaction with auto-commit off:

| Steps | Firebird server | Proxy |
| --- | --- | --- |
| With auto-commit off, `SELECT ID FROM T_PLAIN WHERE ID = 6601` | Success, no rows | Success, no rows |
| `SAVEPOINT S1` | Success | Error [`isc_random`](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/firebird/blob/v5.0.4/src/include/firebird/impl/msg/jrd.h#L63) "Unknown exception." |
| `ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT S1` | Success | Error [`isc_dsql_error`](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/firebird/blob/v5.0.4/src/include/firebird/impl/msg/jrd.h#L250) "Dynamic SQL Error; You have an error in your SQL syntax: ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT S1, null" |
| `RELEASE SAVEPOINT S1` | Success | Error [`isc_dsql_error`](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/firebird/blob/v5.0.4/src/include/firebird/impl/msg/jrd.h#L250) "Dynamic SQL Error; You have an error in your SQL syntax: no viable alternative at input 'RELEASE'" |

So through the proxy an application cannot set, roll back to or release a savepoint.

### Reason analysis

- The rules [`rollback`](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere/blob/01446d4fad48450e7fc3eb3b3631b25d6bf411df/parser/sql/engine/dialect/firebird/src/main/antlr4/imports/firebird/TCLStatement.g4#L30-L32) and [`savepoint`](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere/blob/01446d4fad48450e7fc3eb3b3631b25d6bf411df/parser/sql/engine/dialect/firebird/src/main/antlr4/imports/firebird/TCLStatement.g4#L38-L40) of the Firebird grammar of ShardingSphere have no [`ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT`](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/firebird/blob/v5.0.4/src/dsql/parse.y#L5324-L5327) and no [`RELEASE SAVEPOINT`](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/firebird/blob/v5.0.4/src/dsql/parse.y#L5316-L5321), so the proxy rejects these statements with a syntax error.
- [`FirebirdTCLStatementVisitor#visitSavepoint`](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere/blob/01446d4fad48450e7fc3eb3b3631b25d6bf411df/parser/sql/engine/dialect/firebird/src/main/java/org/apache/shardingsphere/sql/parser/engine/firebird/visitor/statement/type/FirebirdTCLStatementVisitor.java#L58-L60) creates [`SavepointStatement`](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere/blob/01446d4fad48450e7fc3eb3b3631b25d6bf411df/parser/sql/statement/core/src/main/java/org/apache/shardingsphere/sql/parser/statement/core/statement/type/tcl/SavepointStatement.java#L27) with the savepoint name `null`. [`SetSavepointProxyBackendHandler#execute`](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere/blob/01446d4fad48450e7fc3eb3b3631b25d6bf411df/proxy/backend/core/src/main/java/org/apache/shardingsphere/proxy/backend/handler/tcl/local/type/SetSavepointProxyBackendHandler.java#L55-L59) passes this name through [`ProxyBackendTransactionManager#setSavepoint`](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere/blob/01446d4fad48450e7fc3eb3b3631b25d6bf411df/proxy/backend/core/src/main/java/org/apache/shardingsphere/proxy/backend/connector/jdbc/transaction/ProxyBackendTransactionManager.java#L190-L195) and [`ConnectionSavepointManager#setSavepoint`](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere/blob/01446d4fad48450e7fc3eb3b3631b25d6bf411df/kernel/transaction/core/src/main/java/org/apache/shardingsphere/transaction/savepoint/ConnectionSavepointManager.java#L61-L64) to [`FBConnection#setSavepoint(String)`](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/jaybird/blob/v5.0.13/src/main/org/firebirdsql/jdbc/FBConnection.java#L940-L948) of the backend Jaybird. Jaybird [quotes the name](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/jaybird/blob/v5.0.13/src/main/org/firebirdsql/jdbc/FBConnection.java#L908-L909) with [`QuoteStrategy#appendQuoted`](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/jaybird/blob/v5.0.13/src/main/org/firebirdsql/jdbc/QuoteStrategy.java#L48-L50) and fails with `NullPointerException`, and [`SQLExceptionTransformEngine#toSQLException`](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere/blob/01446d4fad48450e7fc3eb3b3631b25d6bf411df/database/exception/core/src/main/java/org/apache/shardingsphere/database/exception/core/SQLExceptionTransformEngine.java#L68) turns it into [`UnknownSQLException`](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere/blob/01446d4fad48450e7fc3eb3b3631b25d6bf411df/infra/common/src/main/java/org/apache/shardingsphere/infra/exception/generic/UnknownSQLException.java#L30-L32) "Unknown exception.".

### Steps to reproduce

#### Environment

- Firebird server 5.0.
- Client: Jaybird 6.0.7.

#### Steps

1. Create the table on the Firebird server:

```sql
CREATE TABLE T_PLAIN (ID INTEGER NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, NAME VARCHAR(20));
```

2. Start ShardingSphere-Proxy with this configuration:

```yaml
# global.yaml
authority:
  users:
    - user: root@%
      password: root
  privilege:
    type: ALL_PERMITTED
props:
  proxy-frontend-database-protocol-type: Firebird
```

```yaml
# database-firebird.yaml
databaseName: firebird_db
dataSources:
  ds_0:
    url: jdbc:firebirdsql://<host>/<database file>?encoding=UTF8
    username: <user>
    password: <password>
rules:
  - !SINGLE
    tables:
      - "*.*"
```

3. Turn auto-commit off, execute the statements from the table under "Actual behavior" in one transaction through the proxy and directly on the Firebird server, and compare the results. "Example code" runs all of them.

### Example code

The statements from the table on Jaybird 6.0.7. The comment above each call shows what it prints against the Firebird server and through the proxy.

```java
import java.sql.Connection;
import java.sql.DriverManager;
import java.sql.ResultSet;
import java.sql.ResultSetMetaData;
import java.sql.SQLException;
import java.sql.Statement;
import java.sql.Types;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class SavepointStatements {

    // The proxy; put the URL, user and password of the Firebird server here to compare
    static final String URL = "jdbc:firebirdsql://<proxy host>:<proxy port>/firebird_db?encoding=UTF8&wireCrypt=DISABLED";

    static final String USER = "root";

    static final String PASSWORD = "root";

    public static void main(final String[] args) throws SQLException {
        try (Connection connection = DriverManager.getConnection(URL, USER, PASSWORD)) {
            connection.setAutoCommit(false);
            // A statement before SAVEPOINT, so that the transaction already uses a backend connection of the proxy
            // Firebird server: "SELECT ID FROM T_PLAIN WHERE ID = 6601 -> ID INTEGER: no rows";
            // proxy: "SELECT ID FROM T_PLAIN WHERE ID = 6601 -> ID INTEGER: no rows"
            execute(connection, "SELECT ID FROM T_PLAIN WHERE ID = 6601");
            // Firebird server: "SAVEPOINT S1 -> update count -1"; proxy: "SAVEPOINT S1 -> error: Unknown exception. More details: java.lang.NullPointerException: Cannot invoke "String.length()" because "objectName" is null [SQLState:HY000, ISC error code:335544382]"
            execute(connection, "SAVEPOINT S1");
            // Firebird server: "ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT S1 -> update count -1";
            // proxy: "ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT S1 -> error: Dynamic SQL Error; You have an error in your SQL syntax: ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT S1, null [SQLState:42000, ISC error code:335544569]"
            execute(connection, "ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT S1");
            // Firebird server: "RELEASE SAVEPOINT S1 -> update count -1";
            // proxy: "RELEASE SAVEPOINT S1 -> error: Dynamic SQL Error; You have an error in your SQL syntax: no viable alternative at input 'RELEASE' [SQLState:42000, ISC error code:335544569]"
            execute(connection, "RELEASE SAVEPOINT S1");
            connection.commit();
        }
    }

    // Executes the statement and prints the described columns with the rows, the update count or the error
    static void execute(final Connection connection, final String sql) {
        try (Statement statement = connection.createStatement()) {
            if (!statement.execute(sql)) {
                System.out.println(sql + " -> update count " + statement.getUpdateCount());
                return;
            }
            try (ResultSet resultSet = statement.getResultSet()) {
                ResultSetMetaData metaData = resultSet.getMetaData();
                List<String> columns = new ArrayList<>();
                for (int i = 1; i <= metaData.getColumnCount(); i++) {
                    int type = metaData.getColumnType(i);
                    boolean text = Types.CHAR == type || Types.VARCHAR == type;
                    columns.add(metaData.getColumnLabel(i) + " " + metaData.getColumnTypeName(i) + (text ? "(" + metaData.getPrecision(i) + ")" : ""));
                }
                List<String> rows = new ArrayList<>();
                while (resultSet.next()) {
                    List<String> values = new ArrayList<>();
                    for (int i = 1; i <= metaData.getColumnCount(); i++) {
                        values.add(String.valueOf(resultSet.getObject(i)));
                    }
                    rows.add(String.join(", ", values));
                }
                System.out.println(sql + " -> " + String.join(", ", columns) + ": " + (rows.isEmpty() ? "no rows" : String.join("; ", rows)));
            }
        } catch (final SQLException ex) {
            System.out.println(sql + " -> error: " + ex.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

## Package version

`@adonisjs/lucid` 22.4.2 (knex 3.3.0, pg 8.23.1), PostgreSQL 18.6. The code involved is unchanged on `22.x`.

## Describe the bug

`TransactionClient.commit()` and `rollback()` await knex's `commit()` and `rollback()`. On PostgreSQL, those resolve even when the transaction did not end the way the caller asked:

1. **A caught error, then COMMIT.** A statement fails inside a transaction, for example a unique violation handled as "email already taken". PostgreSQL has now aborted the transaction and answers `COMMIT` with `ROLLBACK`. knex resolves `commit()` with that reply. Lucid reports success and runs the `after('commit')` hooks. None of the transaction's writes is committed.
2. **The COMMIT fails.** The connection is lost before `COMMIT`. knex resolves `commit()` with `undefined` and records the error on the transaction's `executionPromise`. Lucid reports success and runs the `after('commit')` hooks.
   - When the connection is lost *after* `COMMIT` was sent, Lucid behaves the same. In our simulated test, the server committed and the reply was discarded. The outcome is then genuinely unknown to the client, yet Lucid reported success and ran the hooks.
3. **A savepoint rollback fails.** A nested `trx.transaction()` throws, and its `ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT` fails on the client. knex resolves `rollback()` and records the failure on the nested transaction's `executionPromise`. Lucid throws the original error, and nothing records that the rollback failed. If the caller handles that error and continues, the outer `COMMIT` succeeds and includes the nested transaction's writes.
4. **A savepoint release runs `after('commit')` hooks.** A nested transaction's hooks run when its savepoint is released (`RELEASE SAVEPOINT`), before the outer transaction has committed. If the outer transaction then rolls back, the hooks have run for writes that were never committed. This needs no fault injection:

   ```js
   await db.transaction(async (trx) => {
     await trx.transaction(async (sp) => {
       sp.after('commit', () => console.log('nested after(commit) ran'))
       await sp.rawQuery(`insert into lucid_outcome values ('case4')`)
     })
     throw new Error('the outer transaction fails')
   }).catch(() => {})
   // prints "nested after(commit) ran"; case4 is not in the table
   ```

   Verified with the same shape in our harness (LH1f). This snippet itself is not part of the script below.

knex's maintainers described this contract in [knex#3847](https://github.com/knex/knex/issues/3847), and knex's transactions guide documents `executionPromise`: `commit()` does not report failures, and the transaction's outcome is read from `trx.executionPromise`. Lucid never reads it.

**Lucid's own write helpers are affected too.** Many model and relation methods open a transaction when none is passed, then call `trx.commit()` through `managedTransaction()` in `src/utils/index.ts`. Examples are `Model.createMany()`, `Model.updateOrCreateMany()`, `user.related('posts').create()` and `saveMany()`. Case 2 therefore applies to them as well, in applications that never call `db.transaction()` (source reading; not run).

## Reproduction

The script uses a standalone `Database`. The same three blocks can run in an AdonisJS app with the `db` service.

```js
// DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/scratch node lucid-transaction-outcome.mjs
import pg from 'pg'
import { Database } from '@adonisjs/lucid/database'

const logger = { trace() {}, debug() {}, info() {}, warn: (...a) => console.log('[warn]', a.find((x) => typeof x === 'string')), error() {}, child() { return logger } }
const db = new Database({ connection: 'pg', connections: { pg: { client: 'pg', connection: { connectionString: process.env.DATABASE_URL }, pool: { min: 0, max: 1 } } } }, logger, { emit() {}, hasListeners() { return false } })
await db.rawQuery('drop table if exists lucid_outcome; create table lucid_outcome (code text primary key)')
await db.rawQuery(`insert into lucid_outcome values ('dup')`)

// 1. A caught unique violation, then the callback returns: PostgreSQL rolls back, Lucid reports success.
const r1 = await db.transaction(async (trx) => {
  trx.after('commit', () => console.log('1. after(commit) hook ran'))
  await trx.rawQuery(`insert into lucid_outcome values ('case1')`)
  await trx.rawQuery(`insert into lucid_outcome values ('dup')`).catch((e) => console.log('1. statement failed with', e.code))
  return 'committed'
}).then((v) => v, (e) => `rejected: ${e.message}`)
console.log('1. db.transaction():', r1)

// 2. The connection is lost before COMMIT: Lucid reports success.
const r2 = await db.transaction(async (trx) => {
  await trx.rawQuery(`insert into lucid_outcome values ('case2')`)
  const { rows: [{ pid }] } = await trx.rawQuery('select pg_backend_pid() as pid')
  const admin = new pg.Client(process.env.DATABASE_URL)
  await admin.connect()
  await admin.query('select pg_terminate_backend($1)', [pid])
  await admin.end()
  await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 200))
  return 'committed'
}).then((v) => v, (e) => `rejected: ${e.message}`)
console.log('2. db.transaction():', r2)

// 3. A nested transaction's ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT fails on the client (simulated once); the caller handles the error.
const original = pg.Client.prototype.query
pg.Client.prototype.query = function (config, ...rest) {
  const text = typeof config === 'string' ? config : config.text
  if (/^ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT/i.test(text)) {
    pg.Client.prototype.query = original
    const cb = rest.find((a) => typeof a === 'function')
    const err = new Error('simulated client-side failure')
    if (cb) { process.nextTick(() => cb(err)); return }
    return Promise.reject(err)
  }
  return original.call(this, config, ...rest)
}
const r3 = await db.transaction(async (trx) => {
  await trx.rawQuery(`insert into lucid_outcome values ('case3-outer')`)
  try {
    await trx.transaction(async (sp) => {
      await sp.rawQuery(`insert into lucid_outcome values ('case3-inner')`)
      throw new Error('inner business error')
    })
  } catch (e) {
    console.log('3. caller caught:', e.message)
  }
  return 'committed'
}).then((v) => v, (e) => `rejected: ${e.message}`)
pg.Client.prototype.query = original
console.log('3. db.transaction():', r3)

console.log('committed rows:', (await db.rawQuery('select code from lucid_outcome order by code')).rows.map((r) => r.code).join(', '))
await db.rawQuery('drop table lucid_outcome')
await db.manager.closeAll()
```

Output:

```text
1. statement failed with 23505
1. after(commit) hook ran
1. db.transaction(): committed
2. db.transaction(): committed
[warn] Connection Error: Connection ended unexpectedly
3. caller caught: inner business error
3. db.transaction(): committed
committed rows: case3-inner, case3-outer, dup
```

- `case1` and `case2` were not committed, yet both transactions resolved.
- `case3-inner` was committed, although its transaction threw and the caller was told so.

## Expected behavior

- Cases 1 and 2: `db.transaction()` (and `trx.commit()` in the manual form) rejects, and `after('commit')` hooks do not run.
  - When the connection was lost after `COMMIT` was sent, the error should say that the outcome is unknown, not that the transaction rolled back.
- Case 3: the failed rollback is reported. The enclosing transaction refuses to commit, because the savepoint's writes may still be in it.
- Case 4: a nested transaction's `after('commit')` hooks wait for the top-level `COMMIT`, and are dropped if the savepoint or the outer transaction rolls back.

## Proposed change

This would change behaviour. A callback that catches an error and returns normally resolves today, and it would reject. Those writes are already lost. I have not opened a pull request. I would like to know whether this approach is the one you want before doing that.

The change is about 30 lines in `src/transaction_client/index.ts`. `QueryClient.transaction()` needs the same `catch` change.

```ts
async commit() {
  // NEW: a nested transaction of this one could not be rolled back
  if (this.rollbackFailure) {
    this.removeAllListeners()
    throw new E_ROLLBACK_FAILED(this.rollbackFailure) // "refusing to commit"
  }
  try {
    const result = await this.knexClient.commit()
    // NEW: knex resolves commit() even when COMMIT fails; the failure is on executionPromise (knex#3847)
    const failure = await this.knexClient.executionPromise.then(() => null, (e) => e)
    if (failure) throw new E_COMMIT_FAILED(failure)
    // NEW: PostgreSQL answers COMMIT in an aborted transaction with the command tag ROLLBACK
    if (result?.response?.command === 'ROLLBACK') throw new E_COMMIT_ROLLED_BACK()
    this.emit('commit', this)
    this.removeAllListeners()
  } catch (error) {
    this.removeAllListeners()
    throw error
  }
  // after:commit hooks unchanged; they no longer run when the commit failed
}

async rollback() {
  const alreadyCompleted = this.isCompleted // NEW: e.g. after a failed COMMIT
  try {
    await this.knexClient.rollback()
    // NEW: knex resolves rollback() even when ROLLBACK fails
    const failure = alreadyCompleted ? null : await this.knexClient.executionPromise.then(() => null, (e) => e)
    if (failure) {
      if (this.parentTransaction) this.parentTransaction.rollbackFailure ??= failure // NEW
      throw new E_ROLLBACK_FAILED(failure)
    }
    this.emit('rollback', this)
    this.removeAllListeners()
  } catch (error) {
    this.removeAllListeners()
    throw error
  }
  // after:rollback hooks unchanged
}

// In transaction(): set `transaction.parentTransaction = this` on the nested client. In the managed form's catch, keep
// the original error, for example as `originalError` on the rollback error, or the rollback error as the original's `cause`.
```

- **Where the result comes from.** `command === 'ROLLBACK'` is what pg returns for a `COMMIT` that PostgreSQL rolled back, and knex passes that result through `commit()`.
- **Other dialects.** The `executionPromise` checks are dialect-neutral. The command check is PostgreSQL-specific. Other dialects can skip it, or use their own signal.
- **Two refinements**, found by testing the simulated change:
  - **Nested hooks.** Without a change, the nested hooks of case 4 still run at the release. Moving a nested transaction's commit hooks to its parent on release, and dropping them on rollback, fixed it in our simulation. It still ran them after a successful top-level commit.
  - **Unknown outcomes.** The change above reports a lost reply as `E_COMMIT_FAILED`, although the server had committed. An error from `COMMIT` that carries no SQLSTATE should be reported as "outcome unknown" instead. A failure the server reported, or a `ROLLBACK` reply, is a definite failure.
- **Simulation.** We simulated exactly this change by replacing the three methods at runtime. It passed the three cases above, two levels of nesting, the manual form, and a regression run over nested commits, caught nested rollbacks, both hooks, isolation levels and connection reuse. It produced no unhandled rejections.
- **What it cannot do.** When a top-level `ROLLBACK` fails, the connection still returns to the pool with its transaction open. The next transaction on that connection then commits the failed one's writes. Releasing the connection is knex's job. That is reported separately as [knex#6546](https://github.com/knex/knex/issues/6546).
  - **Until knex fixes it,** a `pool.validate` that refuses any connection whose `client.getTransactionStatus()` (pg 8.21+) is not `'I'` prevents the reuse at no measurable cost.
  - **Lucid could make that the PostgreSQL default.** That is a separate discussion.

## Tests

One test per case, against PostgreSQL:

1. **Caught duplicate key.** Insert a duplicate key inside `db.transaction()` and catch the error. Assert:
   - the transaction rejects with `E_COMMIT_ROLLED_BACK`;
   - the `after('commit')` hook did not run;
   - the row is absent.
2. **Connection lost before return.** Terminate the transaction's backend with `pg_terminate_backend()` from a second connection, before returning. This needs the same role or `pg_signal_backend`. Assert:
   - the transaction rejects with `E_COMMIT_FAILED`;
   - the `after('commit')` hook did not run.
3. **Failed savepoint rollback.** Make one `ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT` fail, as in the script, inside a nested transaction whose caller catches and returns. Assert:
   - the outer transaction rejects with `E_ROLLBACK_FAILED`;
   - the inner row is absent.
4. **No false positive.** A nested transaction throws and rolls back normally, and its caller catches and returns. Assert:
   - the outer transaction commits;
   - only the outer row is present;
   - no error is raised.

   This guards the change against failing ordinary code.
5. **Nested hooks.** Register `after('commit')` on a nested transaction, let it be released, then make the outer transaction throw. Assert that the hook did not run. Repeat with the outer committing, and assert that the hook ran once, after the commit.
6. **Lost reply.** Make one `COMMIT` reach the server and then fail on the client: run it, discard the reply, and close the connection. Assert that the error says the outcome is unknown, and that no hook ran.
7. **Managed helper.** Make one `COMMIT` fail, with the same stub as test 3 but matching `COMMIT`, during `Model.createMany()` called without a transaction. Assert that it rejects with `E_COMMIT_FAILED`.

Also keep the existing transaction tests green. Nested commits, caught nested rollbacks, hooks and isolation levels must behave as before.

## Summary

With the postgres-js driver, a refusal TypeGraph recovers from inside a transaction still fails that transaction at commit. TypeGraph rolls a fused write program back to a raw `SAVEPOINT` when one of its statements aborts on purpose (for example a lost unique claim), then raises the typed refusal. postgres-js's `sql.begin` records the first failed query of the transaction and rethrows it when the callback returns, unless the savepoint was opened through `sql.savepoint()`. The caller catches the typed error, but the transaction then rejects with the recovered driver error and nothing it wrote commits.

## Reproduction (main, 1672a5e8)

```ts
const sql = postgres(url);
const backend = createPostgresBackend(drizzle(sql));
const Stamp = defineNode("Stamp", { schema: z.object({ label: z.string() }) });
const graph = defineGraph({
  id: "pgjs_savepoint",
  nodes: { Stamp: { type: Stamp, unique: [{ name: "label", fields: ["label"], scope: "kind", collation: "binary" }] } },
  edges: {},
});
const [store] = await createStoreWithSchema(graph, backend);
const first = await store.nodes.Stamp.create({ label: "one" });
const second = await store.nodes.Stamp.create({ label: "two" });
await store.nodes.Stamp.create({ label: "taken" });

await store.transaction(async (tx) => {
  try {
    await tx.nodes.Stamp.bulkReplaceById([
      { id: first.id, props: { label: "renamed" } },
      { id: second.id, props: { label: "taken" } },
    ]);
  } catch (error) {
    // UniquenessError, as expected
  }
});
// rejects: PostgresError 23502 null value in column "created_at" of relation "typegraph_nodes"
```

The statement log is `SAVEPOINT typegraph_atomic_program`, the claim and write statements, the program's deliberate aborting insert, `ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT typegraph_atomic_program`, `RELEASE SAVEPOINT`, the diagnostic reads, then `rollback` issued by postgres-js. node-postgres commits the same transaction.

## Scope

Every place TypeGraph recovers through a raw savepoint on a caller-visible session is affected on postgres-js: the session atomic program (`src/backend/drizzle/execution/session-atomic-batch.ts`), recorded-capture savepoints (`src/store/recorded-capture.ts`), and the schema-adoption probe (`src/backend/drizzle/postgres.ts`). The fix is to open those savepoints through the driver's own savepoint scope on postgres-js (drizzle's nested `tx.transaction` maps to `sql.savepoint`), or to declare the driver unable to recover a failed statement and refuse the recovery paths there with a typed error.

The shared integration tests `bulkReplaceById keeps no earlier update when a later one loses a unique key` and `bulkReplaceById creates nothing when an update in the same batch loses a unique key` (`tests/backends/integration/batch-write-verdicts.ts`) reproduce it and are skipped on the postgres-js lane until this is fixed.

## As is

SQLite が `SQLITE_FULL` などでトランザクション全体を自動で巻き戻したあと、呼び出し側がそのエラーを `try/catch` で捕捉してコールバックを最後まで走らせると、knex が最後に発行する `COMMIT` が `no transaction is active` で失敗する。`db.transaction()` はこの `COMMIT` の失敗で reject される。

#482 の修正で、自動ロールバック後の `ROLLBACK` / `ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT` は `LibsqlDialect` が成功として扱うようになった。`COMMIT` は同じ状況（接続の `inTransaction` が `false`）でも実行されるので、この経路だけは「SQLite が実際に失敗した理由」を持たないエラーが届く。

### 最小の再現（macOS、Node v26.7.0、darwin-arm64、knex 3.3.0、libsql 0.5.29）

一時ディレクトリのファイル DB を `LibsqlDialect`（`pool: { min: 1, max: 1 }`）で開き、次を実行する。

```ts
await db.raw('CREATE TABLE t (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, v TEXT)');
await db.raw('PRAGMA cache_size = 10');
await db.raw('PRAGMA max_page_count = 200');
await db.transaction(async (trx) => {
	try {
		for (let i = 0; i < 5000; i++) {
			await trx.raw('INSERT INTO t (v) VALUES (?)', ['x'.repeat(500)]);
		}
	} catch (error) {
		console.log('caught inside:', error.code);
	}
});
```

観察した出力:

```
caught inside: SQLITE_FULL
caller got SQLITE_ERROR COMMIT; - cannot commit - no transaction is active
rows [ { c: 0 } ]
```

内側の `catch` には元の `SQLITE_FULL` が届く。外側の `db.transaction()` は `COMMIT; - cannot commit - no transaction is active`（`code: 'SQLITE_ERROR'`）で reject され、`t` は 0 行のまま。失敗は黙って消えず、データも書かれない。

## To be

自動ロールバックされたトランザクションに対する `COMMIT` の失敗が、利用者に原因（トランザクションが SQLite によって自動で巻き戻されたこと）を伝える形で届く。あるいは、`COMMIT` の失敗は現状の `no transaction is active` のままが意図どおりであることを `LibsqlDialect` の JSDoc に明記する。どちらにするかは未決で、この Issue で決める。

## Why

- SQLite は自動ロールバック後の `ROLLBACK` の失敗を「無害」と定めているが、`COMMIT` については同じ節に記述が無い: https://www.sqlite.org/lang_transaction.html §3 "Response To Errors Within A Transaction"。`COMMIT` を成功として扱うと「書き込まれていないデータが成功したように見える」ので、`ROLLBACK` と同じ扱いにはできない。
- 捕捉した側が元のエラーを持っているので、knex に元の例外を上書きされた #482 の症状とは別の経路。

## How

- 原因の場所: `packages/@nitpicker/archive/src/libsql-dialect.ts` の `LibsqlDialect#_query`。スキップするのは `ROLLBACK` / `ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT` だけで、`COMMIT` は `inTransaction === false` でも `super._query` に渡る。
- リポジトリ内の利用者: トランザクションを使う書き込み経路の `catch` は、`replace-console-logs.ts`・`update-page.ts`・`record-redirect.ts` のいずれも `knex.transaction(...)` の外側で捕捉して書き込みキャッシュを消し、再 throw する形で、コールバックの内側で捕捉して続行するものは見当たらない（`db-ops/`・`populate-*`・`transfer/`・`query/viewer-read-model/` の `catch` を確認）。したがって現時点で `COMMIT` 経路に当たる利用者はいない。
- 案: `inTransaction === false` の `COMMIT` を検出して、`SQLITE_FULL` 等で自動ロールバックされたことを示すメッセージのエラーに置き換える。元のエラーは呼び出し側が捕捉済みなので、dialect 側が覚えておく必要がある。実装コストに対して、現時点で当たる利用者がいないので、先に「現状のままを JSDoc に明記する」案と比べて決める。
